      "Language": "rus",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Preliminary adaptation to short-term exposures to immobilization stress prevents to a large extent the depression of contractile function and the lowering of the right atrium myocardium resistance to hypoxia and excess Ca2+ in experimental infarction of the left ventricle. This fact agrees with the concept that disturbance occurring in the nonischemic heart during infarction is caused by stress-induced damage."},
